{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The claim asserts the expert 'remains more reliable' for tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes, yet its own counterconditions concede two entire classes of such tasks (highly algorithmic ones fully captured by finite rules, and expert-bias-mitigated ones) where non-experts would outperform. 'Remains' is an unconditional superlative that the formulation itself refutes; a countercondition is not a mere edge case but a direct admission the claim is false in those cases, so the claim as stated is over-broad.","counterexample":[{"case":"A structured pathology-screening task with objectively verifiable gold-standard outcomes where a finite checklist captures the diagnostic criteria, and the expert's confirmation bias materially degrades unaided judgment; the expert does not 'remain more reliable' there."}],"target":"2f5dc57d1e0b6bb0ace1a0c42cf5b1f0cdae1702a1a27dace2d921a2c6f10912"}]}